WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.849 --> 00:00:01.849
Pain.

00:00:01.849 --> 00:00:04.929
We know it when we feel it.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.540
Not just because it hurts...

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:09.260
[scream]

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.420
But because it's so subjective

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.380
and hard to quantify.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:14.860
What do do doctors rely on as

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.420
the gold standard to evaluate pain in 2016?

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:19.560
Self assessment.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:21.160
That’s right, a chart that goes from

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:23.180
happy face 0

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.220
to sobbing face 10.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.050
Not exactly precise.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.320
So, why can’t we just plug someone into a machine

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:31.580
and get an objective readout

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.760
of how much pain they’re in?

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.620
Pain is complicated and there’s still a lot

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.520
we don’t know about it.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.920
Here’s what we do know:

00:00:47.059 --> 00:00:51.089
It helps us react quickly if we’re in danger

00:00:51.089 --> 00:00:54.090
and alerts us to injury and illness.

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.089
There might even be a good kind of pain...

00:00:56.089 --> 00:00:58.400
I feel like you know no pain no gain,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.179
the saying rings true for,

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:03.300
you know, working out and like I guess

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.240
trying to achieve a goal.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:06.540
You know, studying hard for a test.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.720
Popping a zit?

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.479
Yeah, a masseuse, that's a good pain.

00:01:12.479 --> 00:01:14.610
I’m like: “Rub that tension away.”

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.100
We can’t talk about that on film [laughs].

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:22.820
We do mostly understand the biochemistry

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:24.080
of how pain signals

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.670
get sent to the brain.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:27.900
We can design drugs that target that chemistry

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.280
to ease pain,

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.300
but we haven’t found a way to look at those signals

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.400
to measure it.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.850
Some mutations in our genes lead to pain disorders

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:37.200
like not being able to feel pain

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.080
or feeling excessive amounts.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.260
But more often, genes and the environment we live in

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.660
both contribute to whether we experience some sort of

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.080
pain disease, like arthritis.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.820
So, pain starts out as a signal registered
by sensors in the body.

00:01:52.409 --> 00:01:54.189
But once the signals get to the brain,

00:01:54.189 --> 00:01:56.509
our expectations can affect how strongly

00:01:56.509 --> 00:01:57.619
we feel that pain.

00:01:57.619 --> 00:01:59.620
Rather than simply passing the signal along,

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:02.020
our higher brain functions meddle in what

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:03.770
we end up feeling.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:06.789
A group of areas in the brain, called the “pain matrix,”

00:02:06.789 --> 00:02:08.200
will light up in an MRI scan

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.440
when an individual is feeling pain.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:12.560
But, recent research shows that the pain matrix

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.540
can be misleading.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.280
Using a group of patients who never feel pain,

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.820
a study found that their pain matrix still activates

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.940
in response to stimuli that healthy people find painful.

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.320
What the pain matrix actually responds to

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.140
is any kind of threat,

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:30.200
whether it’s painful or not.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.260
Researchers are now pursuing what they call

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:34.460
the “neurological pain signature”.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:36.720
They’re counting on high-resolution scanning techniques

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.180
to show what specific parts of the pain matrix

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:43.140
are involved in feeling actual pain.

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.810
They also want to learn how the brain separates

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:47.650
immediate pain from chronic pain.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.060
Scientists are hoping that someday they will have a

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:51.340
pattern of brain activation

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.520
that corresponds exactly to pain.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:56.860
Our brains will always make pain

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.500
a unique experience for each of us.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.970
But perhaps in the future, our understanding of pain

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:05.830
can be more precise than sobbing face 10.

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:10.180
On the pain scale, where do you think the worst pain

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.180
you've ever felt, falls?

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:16.360
I say zero, but I know have a high tolerance for pain.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:16.860
 

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.320
10, child birth, without any medication.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.380
I've always wondered how does my sense of pain

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.060
compare to other people's sense of pain.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:32.600
And that would kind of, if they could say this hurts

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:35.020
as much as breaking my leg.

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:36.720
Eight pounds.

